{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794781047437,"sessionID":"ses_e1ebd75ccffe6y3ELjfB5p5w0O","part":{"partID":"prt_1e14966ba001EMmBRCSLSeWhnX","sessionID":"ses_e1ebd75ccffe6y3ELjfB5p5w0O","messageID":"msg_1e148f711001ssq5WDVm2PcftL","type":"tool","id":"call_c1664c1f12ee438d9074adb0","tool":"shell","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"cd /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research && git -c core.fsmonitor=false diff -U3 2a1a42263cc d21dbc1cafe -- .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy/run-command-behaviour.mjs .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy/command-offer.mjs .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy/README.md"},"output":"diff --git a/.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy/README.md b/.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy/README.md\nindex 1d676a72aed..ea44418e70e 100644\n--- a/.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy/README.md\n+++ b/.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy/README.md\n@@ -108,7 +108,7 @@ A typed command travels on its owner. It is delivered only when the leading reco\n \n `--set fresh` reads `behaviour-fresh-prompts.jsonl` beside the script, a file the capture never publishes. The set refuses an absent file with `fresh_missing`, a row whose split is not `fresh` with `fresh_split`, an id that does not start `fresh-` with `fresh_id`, a file that does not hold 36 rows with `set_short`, and a prompt that repeats another row once case and whitespace are folded with `repeated_row`. A fresh row runs only under arm `off` or `on` (`fresh_arm`) and only with `--run` (`fresh_run`).\n \n-`prepare-behaviour-arena.mjs` leaves a `CLAUDE.md` holding `@AGENTS.md` in an arena that has none and appends `/CLAUDE.md` to `.git/info/exclude`, so Claude Code imports the arena's own rules while the clean-tree check and the head stay as they were.\n+`prepare-behaviour-arena.mjs` leaves a `CLAUDE.md` holding `@AGENTS.md` in an arena that has none and appends `/CLAUDE.md` to `.git/info/exclude`, so Claude Code imports the arena's own rules while the clean-tree check and the head stay as they were. Before it copies anything it refuses the source with `source_dirty` when a tracked or untracked edit would leave the arena unlike the commit it names, with `stale_build` when a copied `dist` is older than the newest tracked file in the folder that holds it or in a folder its tsconfig `include` reaches outside that folder, and with `symlink_escape` when a link inside a copied tree resolves outside the source or names an absolute path that still resolves inside it. It writes the marker as the head, then `source clean`, then one `built <relPath> <ISO time>` line per copied build tree and one `tracked-dotenv <path>` line per tracked dotenv file.\n \n A summary pairs the two arms row by row. `--pair off,on` gives a runtime a `paired` block when both arms ran and either one scored an owned row, and a row whose arms scored a different number of runs, including a row one arm never scored, adds to `unpaired` and to no other count. `pooled` sums those blocks over every runtime and sets `p` to the exact one-sided sign test over `gained` and `lost`, rounded to four decimals and null when no pair moved either way. A reader that walks every top-level key of the summary must skip `pooled`, because it is not a runtime.\n \n@@ -134,13 +134,13 @@ Each arm prints `declineStarts` and `skillOnlyStarts` as `x of y`, plus one line\n | `capture-command-routing-baseline.mjs` | Scores `command-prompts.jsonl` under the pinned regime and prints or writes the baseline. Its `attachedMetrics` key holds own-row, attached and combined plain reach per split and per command family, attached false offers, the typed rows where a command and its owner skill read as ambiguous, and the command attached to each published row. An attached hit counts only on a row the ranked bridge did not already land. Its `rows`, `attachedRows` and `typedRows` maps leave out every holdout row, so only holdout counts are published. The optional `--real-embedding` flag scores the same corpus against this machine's own skill graph database and live embedder in place of the fixture vectors and word overlap the default run substitutes, so its numbers describe an environment rather than a reproducible projection. It refuses `--write` because the committed baseline comes only from the fixture regime. Before scoring it proves two things, that a live embedder answers and serves the `nomic-embed-text` model family the stored vectors were built with, and that the skill graph database holds a populated `vec_cmd_*` command vector table. A missing piece exits 2 with a message that names it. Each row in its `rows` output also carries `offered`, which is true when any of the top three recommendations maps to a command bridge, so it measures reach rather than whether a brief head offered the command. Its `attachedMetrics.needs_mode_floor` counts the silent rows whose head would read `(needs a mode)`, and `needsModeRows` lists every row id whose head would, which happens only when the prompt names the hub that tops it. `attachedMetrics.plain_stages` holds one entry per plain split with seven counts, `total`, `own`, `ownerLed`, `routed`, `withCommand`, `attached` and `neither`, where `own`, `ownerLed` and `neither` split the rows and the counted stages nest from `ownerLed` down to `attached`. `attachedMetrics.fit` holds the two fit classes, `skillOnly` and `decline`, each with `carried` and `total`, and `decline` also carries `byKind` for its decline kinds, so a fit row is counted apart from every recall, reach, silence and agreement tally and reaches no row map. A fit row is frozen once captured, and no later edit, replacement or removal is allowed. Three of the eight slash-variant rows that name a command ask a question about it, and a question about a command carries no command by design, so `slash_variant.reached_top3` can reach 5 of 8. |\n | `score-command-transcripts.mjs` | Scores captured sessions through the shared session readers. `--summary <run-log> [<run-log>...]` scores every row one or more run logs list, per runtime and arm, and `--runtime <name> --locator '<json>'` scores the one session a locator names. Under `--summary`, a Vibe run is read a second time through the Vibe reader, because its tool calls live in the journal entries and not in the records every reader shares, and its reads, writes and shell steps are the `file_system.read_file`, `file_system.write_file`, `file_system.edit` and `file_system.bash` calls its journal records. `--row-id <id>` narrows `--summary` to one row and is a usage error with `--runtime`. A summary keeps one line per runtime, arm, row and run, so two runs of one row are two samples while a retried line still replaces the first line of its own run. A run whose arm is `off` and whose `briefDelivered` is `true`, or whose arm is `on` and whose `briefDelivered` is `false`, is left unscored as `arm_delivery_mismatch`, because it measured the other arm. `--json` prints machine-readable output. `SKILL_ADVISOR_FRESH_PROMPTS_PATH` points the label reader at another fresh row file, which lets a test label made-up rows before the blind file exists, and no arm or scoring command sets it. A run line whose arm is `paired` is filed under the `unknown` arm as one whose arm is `unscored` is, because the summary already spends both words on a key. `--pair <from>,<to>` compares two arms of one summary run row by row and adds a `paired` block, holding `from`, `to`, `rows`, `gained`, `lost`, `bothFollowed`, `neitherFollowed` and `unpaired`, to a runtime whose two arms both ran and either of which scored an owned row, and the text output prints one `paired` line for it. `--pair` without `--summary` exits 2 with `--pair needs --summary`, and one arm name exits 2 with `--pair needs two arm names`. A row whose arms scored a different number of runs, including a row one arm never scored, adds to `unpaired` and to no other count. `pooled` sums the `paired` blocks of every runtime and holds `from`, `to`, `runtimes`, `rows`, `gained`, `lost`, `ties`, `unpaired` and `p`, where `p` is the exact one-sided sign test over `gained` and `lost`, rounded to four decimals and null when no pair moved either way. It also holds `declineStarts` and `skillOnlyStarts` per arm as `started` and `runs`, summed over every runtime that ran the arm. `pooled` is not a runtime, so a reader that walks every top-level key of the summary must skip it. Each per-arm block reports `startRate`, `cappedStartRows`, `offered`, `cappedOfferRows`, `startedOfOffered`, `offerReached`, `deliveryUnknown`, `offerSeen`, `offerDeclined`, `offerFieldAbsent`, `unsafe`, `unsafeNoChangedPath`, `armIncomplete`, `byPolicy`, `workflow`, `declineStarts`, `skillOnlyStarts` and `commandStartRuns`, where `byPolicy` holds a `run`, `start` and `ask` tally of rows, capped rows, started rows and rows that wrote. The `workflow` block sorts each run into `command`, `skill`, `improvised`, `none` or `noOwner` and prints `share` as the runs that loaded the command's or the owning skill's own workflow before their first write, out of every run that acted. It shows a workflow was loaded, not that each step was followed. The block also carries `noneActed`, the `none` runs that read a file or ran a shell step, and `loadRate`, the runs that loaded the command's or the owning skill's workflow out of every scored run with an owner, which is the outcome reading because a run that skipped the workflow and wrote nothing cannot leave its count. The block also holds `asked`, an ask row whose run offered the command and wrote nothing, `askedActed`, the `asked` runs that also worked the task before they asked, `otherMode`, a load of a different mode under the owning hub, and `followedRate`, which is `command + skill + asked` over every owned scored run. `loadRate` has the same denominator, the six path counts, so no owned scored run leaves it. `followedRange` and `loadRange` sit beside those two rates as their Wilson interval at 1.96, rounded to two decimals and clamped to 0 and 1, so a small arm is not read as a precise rate, and both are null when the denominator is zero. `followedOffered` and `followedNotOffered` read the same followed count inside each group the offer puts a run in, and `offerUnknown` counts an owned run whose line holds no offer field, so a gap in the record is never read as no offer. A load is a read tool, a skill tool, a shell command that reads the file or a `prompts/<namespace>-<name>.md` mirror, and a write outside the run's checkout sets no boundary. A Codex shell step arrives as the JavaScript body of an `exec` call, and its command is the `cmd` strings of that body, one per line. A body that holds a gate lookup and a task command reads as a gate step. A search with `rg` or `grep` is not a load, a line inside a heredoc is not a command, and a bare document name is read in the folder an earlier `cd` of the same command entered. A start and a loop the run took are read from the same reads a load is, so a `cat` of the command's document counts and a search, an in-place `sed` or a heredoc line does not. A heredoc's end word may hold a hyphen or a dot. A command that sat on the partner side of an ambiguous head is counted in `offeredAsPartner` and not in `offered`. A run line stored before the writers kept `offeredAs` holds none, so its offer counts as before. The rate counts a workflow opened before the first write, not each step followed. `cappedOfferRows` counts offers whose turn hit its cap, `offerReached` counts offers whose brief was not shown missing, so a row whose brief state is unknown counts, `deliveryUnknown` counts every run line whose brief state is `unknown`, scored or not, `offerSeen` and `offerDeclined` read the session's `COMMAND:` lines, and `offerFieldAbsent` counts rows that carry no offer field. `unsafe` also counts a workflow loop that started without its init, `unsafeNoChangedPath` counts the unsafe runs whose run line lists no changed path, apart from a loop without init, and a Claude turn that keeps only the `<command-name>` tag of a typed command counts as a start because Claude Code expands that command before the model sees it. A write to a git-ignored path or one the model reverted itself also leaves no changed path. The text output prints one `policy` line per policy under each arm. A decline or skill-only run started a tracked command when a skill call named it, a `COMMAND:` line reported it `started`, the host expanded it, or the run read the command's document and also wrote inside its checkout. A read with no write is a lookup and not a start, and a search, an in-place `sed` or a heredoc line is not a read, so `declineStarts` and `skillOnlyStarts` print as `x of y` over those runs and `commandStartRuns` lists each start with its row, run, command and offered command. Exit 2 means nothing was scored: bad flags, a missing or malformed run log (`run_log_missing`, `run_log_malformed`), or a reader refusal printed with its own code such as `capability_gated` or `locator_unavailable`. |\n | `command-offer.mjs` | Recovers the attribution fields for a run line. `parseOffer` reads the command and policy out of one head, `replayOffer` replays a checkout's Claude prompt hook after the turn, `briefDelivered` reads the session record, and `backfillRunLog` writes the fields onto a finished log with a refusal code for a holdout, unknown or same-path input, after `trustedCheckout` confirms each line's checkout is the project root or a marked arena. Each offer carries `offeredAs`, `head` or `partner`. A fallback head such as `Advisor: no skill matched.` counts as a delivered brief with a null offer, and a head quoted inside a tool result does not count. The offer on a stored run of a runtime other than Claude is the Claude hook's replay, recorded when the run was made, and no rescore rewrites it. |\n-| `run-command-behaviour.mjs` | Runs one live turn per row and records the locator, exit and changed paths. `--print-argv <runtime>` prints a runtime's argv, `--list <set>` prints a set's row ids, and a run takes `--runtime` with `--set` or repeated `--row-id`, `--out <run-log>`, optional `--arm`, `--fixture` and `--dry-run`. Without `--arm`, a selection whose ids are all started-command rows runs under arm `g2`, one that holds none runs under `pre` and a mix is refused with `mixed_row_arms`. The `off` and `on` arms compare one commit with the advisor switched off and on, where a turn under either drops every inherited name that ends `_DISABLED` and holds `SKILL_ADVISOR` or `HOOKS`, and the `off` arm then sets `SYSTEM_SKILL_ADVISOR_HOOK_DISABLED` and `SYSTEM_SKILL_ADVISOR_PLUGIN_DISABLED` to `1`. `--run <n>` takes a whole number from 1 and records it on every line of that run, so two samples of one row stay apart. The `fresh` set reads `behaviour-fresh-prompts.jsonl` beside the script and refuses it with `fresh_missing`, `fresh_id`, `fresh_split`, `set_short`, `repeated_row`, `fresh_arm` or `fresh_run` when the file or a row breaks its rule. Each `g2` run line records the `fixture` folder its prompt named, and `--backfill` replays that folder, or `g2-fixture` beside the log for a line that holds none. `--backfill <run-log> --out <new-log> [--brief-only]` rewrites a finished log with `offeredCommand`, `offeredPolicy`, `offeredAs` and `briefDelivered`, or only `briefDelivered` with `--brief-only`, instead of running a turn, and prints a JSON refusal with `same_path`, `holdout_row` or `unknown_row` when it cannot. After each turn it restores the checkout to HEAD and reports any `leftoverPaths` whose status still differs from the snapshot taken before the turn. Exit 3 (`head_moved`) ends the arm without that restore, because a commit or a stash made during the turn is not the runner's to discard, so the line records `restored: false` and the changed paths stay listed. A live turn gets only the allowlisted environment, the shell basics, the locale and XDG prefixes, each runtime's own prefixes and the Skilled switch prefixes, plus the GitHub and git overrides, so no GitHub token or git credential helper from the operator's shell reaches it. Exit 2 refuses instead of running: bad flags, an unknown runtime or set, a holdout or unknown row, a set with fewer rows than it needs (`set_short`), a dirty worktree outside the run log's folder (`worktree_dirty`), a checkout with a git remote (`remote_present`), a checkout without the arena builder's marker (`arena_unprepared`), a selection that mixes started-command rows with others (`mixed_row_arms`), or a Codex arm whose project hook is unapproved (`codex-hooks-unapproved`). Cursor and Vibe are named as not measured, so `--print-argv` or `--runtime` for either exits 2 with its reason rather than `unknown runtime`. |\n+| `run-command-behaviour.mjs` | Runs one live turn per row and records the locator, exit and changed paths. `--print-argv <runtime>` prints a runtime's argv, `--list <set>` prints a set's row ids, and a run takes `--runtime` with `--set` or repeated `--row-id`, `--out <run-log>`, optional `--arm`, `--fixture` and `--dry-run`. Without `--arm`, a selection whose ids are all started-command rows runs under arm `g2`, one that holds none runs under `pre` and a mix is refused with `mixed_row_arms`. The `off` and `on` arms compare one commit with the advisor switched off and on, where a turn under either drops every inherited name that ends `_DISABLED` and holds `SKILL_ADVISOR` or `HOOKS`, and the `off` arm then sets `SYSTEM_SKILL_ADVISOR_HOOK_DISABLED` and `SYSTEM_SKILL_ADVISOR_PLUGIN_DISABLED` to `1`. `--run <n>` takes a whole number from 1 and records it on every line of that run, so two samples of one row stay apart. The `fresh` set reads `behaviour-fresh-prompts.jsonl` beside the script and refuses it with `fresh_missing`, `fresh_id`, `fresh_split`, `set_short`, `repeated_row`, `fresh_arm` or `fresh_run` when the file or a row breaks its rule. Each `g2` run line records the `fixture` folder its prompt named, and `--backfill` replays that folder, or `g2-fixture` beside the log for a line that holds none. `--backfill <run-log> --out <new-log> [--brief-only]` rewrites a finished log with `offeredCommand`, `offeredPolicy`, `offeredAs` and `briefDelivered`, or only `briefDelivered` with `--brief-only`, instead of running a turn, and prints a JSON refusal with `same_path`, `holdout_row` or `unknown_row` when it cannot. After each turn it restores the checkout to HEAD and reports any `leftoverPaths` whose status still differs from the snapshot taken before the turn. Exit 3 (`head_moved`) ends the arm without that restore, because a commit or a stash made during the turn is not the runner's to discard, so the line records `restored: false` and the changed paths stay listed. A live turn gets only the allowlisted environment, the shell basics, the locale and XDG prefixes, each runtime's own prefixes and the Skilled switch prefixes, plus the GitHub and git overrides, so no GitHub token or git credential helper from the operator's shell reaches it, and it runs with `GIT_ALLOW_PROTOCOL=file` so SSH and HTTPS stay out of reach. A live run checks for a remote before every turn, so a remote one turn adds stops the next turn rather than outliving it. Exit 2 refuses instead of running: bad flags, an unknown runtime or set, a holdout or unknown row, a set with fewer rows than it needs (`set_short`), a dirty worktree outside the run log's folder (`worktree_dirty`), a checkout with a git remote (`remote_present`), a checkout without the arena builder's marker (`arena_unprepared`), a selection that mixes started-command rows with others (`mixed_row_arms`), or a Codex arm whose project hook is unapproved (`codex-hooks-unapproved`). Cursor and Vibe are named as not measured, so `--print-argv` or `--runtime` for either exits 2 with its reason rather than `unknown runtime`. |\n | `command-routing-baseline.json` | Captured command routing numbers that `tests/command-routing-slice.vitest.ts` holds the scorer to. |\n | `build-holdout.mjs` | Builds the frozen holdout from three separately authored fixtures that are disjoint from the labeled corpus, copying each row's own label and keeping its provenance in `source_type` and `origin_fixture`. Family-normalized dedup keeps the first occurrence and drops any prompt that collides with the training corpus. `--write` rewrites `holdout-prompts.jsonl`. |\n | `capture-local-native-divergence-ledger.mjs` | Recomputes the strict Python/native top-1 divergence set the ratchet replays. An existing approval is kept only while its prompt hash and both current tops still match, so the output is a capture of live scoring rather than a hand-edited fixture. Scores both scorers against a skill graph indexed fresh into a temporary directory with the clock pinned to the capture day, and `--write` records the result. |\n | `capture-scorer-eval-baseline.mjs` | Captures scorer accuracy across the full corpus, the independent holdout, the frozen ambiguity slice and the named intent buckets under the reproducible filesystem projection, then `--write` records `scorer-eval-baseline.json`. Fixture hashes pin the baseline to the exact corpora it was captured against, so editing a fixture forces a conscious re-capture. `--misses` prints one failing prompt per line. |\n | `derive-ambiguity-slice.mjs` | Derives the frozen ambiguity slice as the corpus rows whose top-2 fused-score margin is at or below `tau`, keeping each row's existing gold label, so a regression that only hurts contested prompts still fails a gate. `--write` rewrites `ambiguity-prompts.jsonl`, and the frozen `margin_at_capture` documents why a row is in the slice without being trusted by the gate. |\n-| `prepare-behaviour-arena.mjs` | Builds the arena a live arm runs in: a clone of the branch tip with no remote and no outer repository, plus the ignored build trees copied in and stamped at copy time so the freshness guard reads them as fresh. It refuses a destination inside a repository or one that already exists, and skips every file whose name begins with `.env` so no secret follows the build output in. When the clone holds no `CLAUDE.md`, the builder writes one holding `@AGENTS.md` and appends `/CLAUDE.md` to `.git/info/exclude`, so Claude Code imports the arena's own rules while the clean-tree check and the head stay as they were. |\n+| `prepare-behaviour-arena.mjs` | Builds the arena a live arm runs in: a clone of the branch tip with no remote and no outer repository, plus the ignored build trees copied in and stamped at copy time so the freshness guard reads them as fresh. It refuses a destination inside a repository or one that already exists, and skips every file whose name begins with `.env` so no secret follows the build output in. It refuses `source_dirty` when the source holds a tracked or untracked edit, `stale_build` when a copied `dist` is older than the newest tracked file in the folder that holds it or in a folder its tsconfig `include` reaches outside it, and `symlink_escape` when a link in a copied tree resolves outside the source or names an absolute path that still resolves inside it. A dotenv file the commit already tracks still arrives with the clone, so the output carries a `trackedDotenv` list naming each one and the marker writes a `tracked-dotenv <path>` line for it. The marker otherwise holds the head, `source clean` and one `built <relPath> <ISO time>` line per copied build tree. When the clone holds no `CLAUDE.md`, the builder writes one holding `@AGENTS.md` and appends `/CLAUDE.md` to `.git/info/exclude`, so Claude Code imports the arena's own rules while the clean-tree check and the head stay as they were. |\n | `reject-unknown-options.mjs` | Shared argument guard that exits 2 on the first token outside a script's declared option list, because silently ignoring a misspelled flag would run the default behavior and make the no-op look successful. |\n | `score-outcome-rerank.mjs` | Measures whether the outcome-weighted rerank improves routing over the similarity-only ordering, reported as MRR and right-skill@3 rather than @1 alone. The reliability fold is built on a train split of the labeled corpus and accuracy is measured only on a disjoint held-out split, so memorized labels do not transfer. Read-only against the skill graph. |\n | `worktree-restore.mjs` | Restores one measured turn's checkout to the snapshot taken before it, leaving a path that was already dirty alone and dropping a staged path HEAD does not know. A second snapshot after the restore reports what still differs, so the caller fails the run instead of claiming a clean worktree. |\ndiff --git a/.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy/command-offer.mjs b/.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy/command-offer.mjs\nindex 197132e0a84..83d8318d5c7 100644\n--- a/.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy/command-offer.mjs\n+++ b/.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy/command-offer.mjs\n@@ -37,6 +37,7 @@ import { dirname, join, resolve } from 'node:path';\n import { fileURLToPath } from 'node:url';\n \n import { loadSession as loadSharedSession } from '../../../../../hooks/shared/session-readers/index.mjs';\n+import { MARK } from './prepare-behaviour-arena.mjs';\n \n const HOOK_RELATIVE = '.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/dist/hooks/claude/user-prompt-submit.js';\n const ADVISOR_CLI_RELATIVE = '.skilled/bin/skill-advisor.cjs';\n@@ -355,7 +356,7 @@ export function trustedCheckout(checkoutDir) {\n   try {\n     return (\n       realpathSync(checkoutDir) === realpathSync(REPO_ROOT)\n-      || existsSync(join(checkoutDir, '.git', 'skilled-behaviour-arena'))\n+      || existsSync(join(checkoutDir, '.git', MARK))\n     );\n   } catch {\n     return false;\ndiff --git a/.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy/run-command-behaviour.mjs b/.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy/run-command-behaviour.mjs\nindex 2cf89e1df07..945ed4c1701 100644\n--- a/.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy/run-command-behaviour.mjs\n+++ b/.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy/run-command-behaviour.mjs\n@@ -32,11 +32,12 @@\n //\n // A live turn is also a turn that can reach the network, so the runner takes the\n // reach away before the model has it. The child gets none of the GitHub tokens, an\n-// empty GitHub CLI config, no credential helper and no terminal to be prompted on.\n-// A live arm additionally refuses to start in a checkout that has any git remote,\n-// because a command wrongly started there could push, tag or publish something the\n-// arm cannot take back. A dry run spawns nothing, so it keeps running in this\n-// checkout and its guards stay about file paths alone.\n+// empty GitHub CLI config, no credential helper and no terminal to be prompted on,\n+// while Git is allowed only the file transport, so SSH and HTTPS stay out of reach\n+// whatever keys HOME holds. A live arm additionally refuses to start in a checkout\n+// that has any git remote, because a command wrongly started there could push, tag\n+// or publish something the arm cannot take back. A dry run spawns nothing, so it\n+// keeps running in this checkout and its guards stay about file paths alone.\n //\n // Usage:\n //   node run-command-behaviour.mjs --print-argv <runtime>\n@@ -54,6 +55,9 @@ import { fileURLToPath } from 'node:url';\n import { dshProjectKey, dshStoreRoot } from '../../../../../hooks/shared/session-readers/dsh.mjs';\n import { loadSession as loadSharedSession } from '../../../../../hooks/shared/session-readers/index.mjs';\n import { backfillRunLog, briefDelivered, codexHookApproval, promptSessionId, replayOffer } from './command-offer.mjs';\n+// The arena builder writes this file into the checkout it made. Its absence is the\n+// proof that a root is an operator's working tree rather than a disposable arena.\n+import { MARK } from './prepare-behaviour-arena.mjs';\n import { restoreTurn, statusSnapshot } from './worktree-restore.mjs';\n \n const HERE = dirname(fileURLToPath(import.meta.url));\n@@ -518,10 +522,6 @@ function locatorFor(runtime, sessionId, reportedId) {\n // lie, and the restore step trusts that snapshot.\n const GIT_PREFIX = ['-c', 'core.fsmonitor=false'];\n \n-// The arena builder writes this file into the checkout it made. Its absence is the\n-// proof that a root is an operator's working tree rather than a disposable arena.\n-const MARK = 'skilled-behaviour-arena';\n-\n function git(args) {\n   const result = spawnSync('git', [...GIT_PREFIX, ...args], {\n     cwd: REPO_ROOT,\n@@ -607,10 +607,14 @@ function gitSnapshot() {\n   };\n }\n \n-// A remote is the one way a turn reaches outside the checkout, so a live arm\n-// refuses to start when one is listed. The builder's marker then proves the root\n-// came from the arena builder, because only that checkout has no remote, no outer\n-// repository and no dotenv file in its copied build trees.\n+// A remote is what a turn could push to by name, so a live arm refuses to start\n+// when one is listed. It is only half the boundary: the push boundary is childEnv's\n+// empty credential helper, empty GH_CONFIG_DIR, GIT_TERMINAL_PROMPT=0 and\n+// GIT_ALLOW_PROTOCOL=file, because a push to an explicit URL needs no remote at all\n+// and the protocol setting keeps SSH and HTTPS out of reach whatever keys HOME\n+// holds. The builder's marker then proves the root came from the arena builder,\n+// because only that checkout has no remote, no outer repository and no dotenv file\n+// in its copied build trees.\n export function liveArmRefusal(root) {\n   const gitAt = (args) => {\n     const result = spawnSync('git', ['-C', root, ...GIT_PREFIX, ...args], {\n@@ -632,6 +636,23 @@ export function liveArmRefusal(root) {\n   return null;\n }\n \n+/**\n+ * Check whether a turn may start where it would run.\n+ *\n+ * A remote one turn adds outlives that turn, so the check runs before every turn\n+ * rather than before the first one only.\n+ *\n+ * @param {string} root - The checkout the turn runs in.\n+ * @param {{ dryRun: boolean }} options - Whether the turn spawns a model.\n+ * @returns {object | null} The refusal, or null when the turn may start.\n+ */\n+export function turnRefusal(root, { dryRun }) {\n+  // A dry run spawns nothing and keeps running in this checkout, so it has no\n+  // turn to guard.\n+  if (dryRun) return null;\n+  return liveArmRefusal(root);\n+}\n+\n // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n // Prompt source and run log\n // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n@@ -1033,6 +1054,8 @@ export function childEnv(ghConfig, arm) {\n   }\n   env.GH_CONFIG_DIR = ghConfig;\n   env.GIT_TERMINAL_PROMPT = '0';\n+  // Git's network transports would let a turn push with whatever identity HOME holds.\n+  env.GIT_ALLOW_PROTOCOL = 'file';\n   Object.assign(env, GIT_CREDENTIAL_OVERRIDES);\n   return env;\n }\n@@ -1263,16 +1286,18 @@ function writeArgv(runtimeName) {\n // fires, so a broken precondition never spends another model call.\n async function runTurn(job, options, exempt, isFirst) {\n   const before = { status: statusPaths(), ...gitSnapshot() };\n+  // A live turn can push, so it may only start in the arena the builder made: no\n+  // remote it could push to by name and the builder's marker in place. The push\n+  // boundary is childEnv's empty credential helper, empty GH_CONFIG_DIR,\n+  // GIT_TERMINAL_PROMPT=0 and GIT_ALLOW_PROTOCOL=file, because a push to an explicit\n+  // URL needs no remote and the protocol setting keeps SSH and HTTPS out of reach\n+  // whatever keys HOME holds. A dry run spawns nothing and keeps running in this\n+  // checkout.\n+  const refusal = turnRefusal(REPO_ROOT, { dryRun: options.dryRun });\n+  if (refusal !== null) {\n+    return { status: EXIT_REFUSED, refusal };\n+  }\n   if (isFirst) {\n-    // A live turn can push, so it may only start in the arena the builder made: no\n-    // remote to push to and the builder's marker in place. A dry run spawns nothing\n-    // and keeps running in this checkout.\n-    if (!options.dryRun) {\n-      const refusal = liveArmRefusal(REPO_ROOT);\n-      if (refusal !== null) {\n-        return { status: EXIT_REFUSED, refusal };\n-      }\n-    }\n     const dirty = dirtyPaths(before.status, exempt);\n     if (dirty.length > 0) {\n       return {\n","title":"shell","metadata":{"metadata":{"status":"completed","truncated":false,"exit":0},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"diff --git a/.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy/README.md b/.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy/README.md\nindex 1d676a72aed..ea44418e70e 100644\n--- a/.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy/README.md\n+++ b/.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy/README.md\n@@ -108,7 +108,7 @@ A typed command travels on its owner. It is delivered only when the leading reco\n \n `--set fresh` reads `behaviour-fresh-prompts.jsonl` beside the script, a file the capture never publishes. The set refuses an absent file with `fresh_missing`, a row whose split is not `fresh` with `fresh_split`, an id that does not start `fresh-` with `fresh_id`, a file that does not hold 36 rows with `set_short`, and a prompt that repeats another row once case and whitespace are folded with `repeated_row`. A fresh row runs only under arm `off` or `on` (`fresh_arm`) and only with `--run` (`fresh_run`).\n \n-`prepare-behaviour-arena.mjs` leaves a `CLAUDE.md` holding `@AGENTS.md` in an arena that has none and appends `/CLAUDE.md` to `.git/info/exclude`, so Claude Code imports the arena's own rules while the clean-tree check and the head stay as they were.\n+`prepare-behaviour-arena.mjs` leaves a `CLAUDE.md` holding `@AGENTS.md` in an arena that has none and appends `/CLAUDE.md` to `.git/info/exclude`, so Claude Code imports the arena's own rules while the clean-tree check and the head stay as they were. Before it copies anything it refuses the source with `source_dirty` when a tracked or untracked edit would leave the arena unlike the commit it names, with `stale_build` when a copied `dist` is older than the newest tracked file in the folder that holds it or in a folder its tsconfig `include` reaches outside that folder, and with `symlink_escape` when a link inside a copied tree resolves outside the source or names an absolute path that still resolves inside it. It writes the marker as the head, then `source clean`, then one `built <relPath> <ISO time>` line per copied build tree and one `tracked-dotenv <path>` line per tracked dotenv file.\n \n A summary pairs the two arms row by row. `--pair off,on` gives a runtime a `paired` block when both arms ran and either one scored an owned row, and a row whose arms scored a different number of runs, including a row one arm never scored, adds to `unpaired` and to no other count. `pooled` sums those blocks over every runtime and sets `p` to the exact one-sided sign test over `gained` and `lost`, rounded to four decimals and null when no pair moved either way. A reader that walks every top-level key of the summary must skip `pooled`, because it is not a runtime.\n \n@@ -134,13 +134,13 @@ Each arm prints `declineStarts` and `skillOnlyStarts` as `x of y`, plus one line\n | `capture-command-routing-baseline.mjs` | Scores `command-prompts.jsonl` under the pinned regime and prints or writes the baseline. Its `attachedMetrics` key holds own-row, attached and combined plain reach per split and per command family, attached false offers, the typed rows where a command and its owner skill read as ambiguous, and the command attached to each published row. An attached hit counts only on a row the ranked bridge did not already land. Its `rows`, `attachedRows` and `typedRows` maps leave out every holdout row, so only holdout counts are published. The optional `--real-embedding` flag scores the same corpus against this machine's own skill graph database and live embedder in place of the fixture vectors and word overlap the default run substitutes, so its numbers describe an environment rather than a reproducible projection. It refuses `--write` because the committed baseline comes only from the fixture regime. Before scoring it proves two things, that a live embedder answers and serves the `nomic-embed-text` model family the stored vectors were built with, and that the skill graph database holds a populated `vec_cmd_*` command vector table. A missing piece exits 2 with a message that names it. Each row in its `rows` output also carries `offered`, which is true when any of the top three recommendations maps to a command bridge, so it measures reach rather than whether a brief head offered the command. Its `attachedMetrics.needs_mode_floor` counts the silent rows whose head would read `(needs a mode)`, and `needsModeRows` lists every row id whose head would, which happens only when the prompt names the hub that tops it. `attachedMetrics.plain_stages` holds one entry per plain split with seven counts, `total`, `own`, `ownerLed`, `routed`, `withCommand`, `attached` and `neither`, where `own`, `ownerLed` and `neither` split the rows and the counted stages nest from `ownerLed` down to `attached`. `attachedMetrics.fit` holds the two fit classes, `skillOnly` and `decline`, each with `carried` and `total`, and `decline` also carries `byKind` for its decline kinds, so a fit row is counted apart from every recall, reach, silence and agreement tally and reaches no row map. A fit row is frozen once captured, and no later edit, replacement or removal is allowed. Three of the eight slash-variant rows that name a command ask a question about it, and a question about a command carries no command by design, so `slash_variant.reached_top3` can reach 5 of 8. |\n | `score-command-transcripts.mjs` | Scores captured sessions through the shared session readers. `--summary <run-log> [<run-log>...]` scores every row one or more run logs list, per runtime and arm, and `--runtime <name> --locator '<json>'` scores the one session a locator names. Under `--summary`, a Vibe run is read a second time through the Vibe reader, because its tool calls live in the journal entries and not in the records every reader shares, and its reads, writes and shell steps are the `file_system.read_file`, `file_system.write_file`, `file_system.edit` and `file_system.bash` calls its journal records. `--row-id <id>` narrows `--summary` to one row and is a usage error with `--runtime`. A summary keeps one line per runtime, arm, row and run, so two runs of one row are two samples while a retried line still replaces the first line of its own run. A run whose arm is `off` and whose `briefDelivered` is `true`, or whose arm is `on` and whose `briefDelivered` is `false`, is left unscored as `arm_delivery_mismatch`, because it measured the other arm. `--json` prints machine-readable output. `SKILL_ADVISOR_FRESH_PROMPTS_PATH` points the label reader at another fresh row file, which lets a test label made-up rows before the blind file exists, and no arm or scoring command sets it. A run line whose arm is `paired` is filed under the `unknown` arm as one whose arm is `unscored` is, because the summary already spends both words on a key. `--pair <from>,<to>` compares two arms of one summary run row by row and adds a `paired` block, holding `from`, `to`, `rows`, `gained`, `lost`, `bothFollowed`, `neitherFollowed` and `unpaired`, to a runtime whose two arms both ran and either of which scored an owned row, and the text output prints one `paired` line for it. `--pair` without `--summary` exits 2 with `--pair needs --summary`, and one arm name exits 2 with `--pair needs two arm names`. A row whose arms scored a different number of runs, including a row one arm never scored, adds to `unpaired` and to no other count. `pooled` sums the `paired` blocks of every runtime and holds `from`, `to`, `runtimes`, `rows`, `gained`, `lost`, `ties`, `unpaired` and `p`, where `p` is the exact one-sided sign test over `gained` and `lost`, rounded to four decimals and null when no pair moved either way. It also holds `declineStarts` and `skillOnlyStarts` per arm as `started` and `runs`, summed over every runtime that ran the arm. `pooled` is not a runtime, so a reader that walks every top-level key of the summary must skip it. Each per-arm block reports `startRate`, `cappedStartRows`, `offered`, `cappedOfferRows`, `startedOfOffered`, `offerReached`, `deliveryUnknown`, `offerSeen`, `offerDeclined`, `offerFieldAbsent`, `unsafe`, `unsafeNoChangedPath`, `armIncomplete`, `byPolicy`, `workflow`, `declineStarts`, `skillOnlyStarts` and `commandStartRuns`, where `byPolicy` holds a `run`, `start` and `ask` tally of rows, capped rows, started rows and rows that wrote. The `workflow` block sorts each run into `command`, `skill`, `improvised`, `none` or `noOwner` and prints `share` as the runs that loaded the command's or the owning skill's own workflow before their first write, out of every run that acted. It shows a workflow was loaded, not that each step was followed. The block also carries `noneActed`, the `none` runs that read a file or ran a shell step, and `loadRate`, the runs that loaded the command's or the owning skill's workflow out of every scored run with an owner, which is the outcome reading because a run that skipped the workflow and wrote nothing cannot leave its count. The block also holds `asked`, an ask row whose run offered the command and wrote nothing, `askedActed`, the `asked` runs that also worked the task before they asked, `otherMode`, a load of a different mode under the owning hub, and `followedRate`, which is `command + skill + asked` over every owned scored run. `loadRate` has the same denominator, the six path counts, so no owned scored run leaves it. `followedRange` and `loadRange` sit beside those two rates as their Wilson interval at 1.96, rounded to two decimals and clamped to 0 and 1, so a small arm is not read as a precise rate, and both are null when the denominator is zero. `followedOffered` and `followedNotOffered` read the same followed count inside each group the offer puts a run in, and `offerUnknown` counts an owned run whose line holds no offer field, so a gap in the record is never read as no offer. A load is a read tool, a skill tool, a shell command that reads the file or a `prompts/<namespace>-<name>.md` mirror, and a write outside the run's checkout sets no boundary. A Codex shell step arrives as the JavaScript body of an `exec` call, and its command is the `cmd` strings of that body, one per line. A body that holds a gate lookup and a task command reads as a gate step. A search with `rg` or `grep` is not a load, a line inside a heredoc is not a command, and a bare document name is read in the folder an earlier `cd` of the same command entered. A start and a loop the run took are read from the same reads a load is, so a `cat` of the command's document counts and a search, an in-place `sed` or a heredoc line does not. A heredoc's end word may hold a hyphen or a dot. A command that sat on the partner side of an ambiguous head is counted in `offeredAsPartner` and not in `offered`. A run line stored before the writers kept `offeredAs` holds none, so its offer counts as before. The rate counts a workflow opened before the first write, not each step followed. `cappedOfferRows` counts offers whose turn hit its cap, `offerReached` counts offers whose brief was not shown missing, so a row whose brief state is unknown counts, `deliveryUnknown` counts every run line whose brief state is `unknown`, scored or not, `offerSeen` and `offerDeclined` read the session's `COMMAND:` lines, and `offerFieldAbsent` counts rows that carry no offer field. `unsafe` also counts a workflow loop that started without its init, `unsafeNoChangedPath` counts the unsafe runs whose run line lists no changed path, apart from a loop without init, and a Claude turn that keeps only the `<command-name>` tag of a typed command counts as a start because Claude Code expands that command before the model sees it. A write to a git-ignored path or one the model reverted itself also leaves no changed path. The text output prints one `policy` line per policy under each arm. A decline or skill-only run started a tracked command when a skill call named it, a `COMMAND:` line reported it `started`, the host expanded it, or the run read the command's document and also wrote inside its checkout. A read with no write is a lookup and not a start, and a search, an in-place `sed` or a heredoc line is not a read, so `declineStarts` and `skillOnlyStarts` print as `x of y` over those runs and `commandStartRuns` lists each start with its row, run, command and offered command. Exit 2 means nothing was scored: bad flags, a missing or malformed run log (`run_log_missing`, `run_log_malformed`), or a reader refusal printed with its own code such as `capability_gated` or `locator_unavailable`. |\n | `command-offer.mjs` | Recovers the attribution fields for a run line. `parseOffer` reads the command and policy out of one head, `replayOffer` replays a checkout's Claude prompt hook after the turn, `briefDelivered` reads the session record, and `backfillRunLog` writes the fields onto a finished log with a refusal code for a holdout, unknown or same-path input, after `trustedCheckout` confirms each line's checkout is the project root or a marked arena. Each offer carries `offeredAs`, `head` or `partner`. A fallback head such as `Advisor: no skill matched.` counts as a delivered brief with a null offer, and a head quoted inside a tool result does not count. The offer on a stored run of a runtime other than Claude is the Claude hook's replay, recorded when the run was made, and no rescore rewrites it. |\n-| `run-command-behaviour.mjs` | Runs one live turn per row and records the locator, exit and changed paths. `--print-argv <runtime>` prints a runtime's argv, `--list <set>` prints a set's row ids, and a run takes `--runtime` with `--set` or repeated `--row-id`, `--out <run-log>`, optional `--arm`, `--fixture` and `--dry-run`. Without `--arm`, a selection whose ids are all started-command rows runs under arm `g2`, one that holds none runs under `pre` and a mix is refused with `mixed_row_arms`. The `off` and `on` arms compare one commit with the advisor switched off and on, where a turn under either drops every inherited name that ends `_DISABLED` and holds `SKILL_ADVISOR` or `HOOKS`, and the `off` arm then sets `SYSTEM_SKILL_ADVISOR_HOOK_DISABLED` and `SYSTEM_SKILL_ADVISOR_PLUGIN_DISABLED` to `1`. `--run <n>` takes a whole number from 1 and records it on every line of that run, so two samples of one row stay apart. The `fresh` set reads `behaviour-fresh-prompts.jsonl` beside the script and refuses it with `fresh_missing`, `fresh_id`, `fresh_split`, `set_short`, `repeated_row`, `fresh_arm` or `fresh_run` when the file or a row breaks its rule. Each `g2` run line records the `fixture` folder its prompt named, and `--backfill` replays that folder, or `g2-fixture` beside the log for a line that holds none. `--backfill <run-log> --out <new-log> [--brief-only]` rewrites a finished log with `offeredCommand`, `offeredPolicy`, `offeredAs` and `briefDelivered`, or only `briefDelivered` with `--brief-only`, instead of running a turn, and prints a JSON refusal with `same_path`, `holdout_row` or `unknown_row` when it cannot. After each turn it restores the checkout to HEAD and reports any `leftoverPaths` whose status still differs from the snapshot taken before the turn. Exit 3 (`head_moved`) ends the arm without that restore, because a commit or a stash made during the turn is not the runner's to discard, so the line records `restored: false` and the changed paths stay listed. A live turn gets only the allowlisted environment, the shell basics, the locale and XDG prefixes, each runtime's own prefixes and the Skilled switch prefixes, plus the GitHub and git overrides, so no GitHub token or git credential helper from the operator's shell reaches it. Exit 2 refuses instead of running: bad flags, an unknown runtime or set, a holdout or unknown row, a set with fewer rows than it needs (`set_short`), a dirty worktree outside the run log's folder (`worktree_dirty`), a checkout with a git remote (`remote_present`), a checkout without the arena builder's marker (`arena_unprepared`), a selection that mixes started-command rows with others (`mixed_row_arms`), or a Codex arm whose project hook is unapproved (`codex-hooks-unapproved`). Cursor and Vibe are named as not measured, so `--print-argv` or `--runtime` for either exits 2 with its reason rather than `unknown runtime`. |\n+| `run-command-behaviour.mjs` | Runs one live turn per row and records the locator, exit and changed paths. `--print-argv <runtime>` prints a runtime's argv, `--list <set>` prints a set's row ids, and a run takes `--runtime` with `--set` or repeated `--row-id`, `--out <run-log>`, optional `--arm`, `--fixture` and `--dry-run`. Without `--arm`, a selection whose ids are all started-command rows runs under arm `g2`, one that holds none runs under `pre` and a mix is refused with `mixed_row_arms`. The `off` and `on` arms compare one commit with the advisor switched off and on, where a turn under either drops every inherited name that ends `_DISABLED` and holds `SKILL_ADVISOR` or `HOOKS`, and the `off` arm then sets `SYSTEM_SKILL_ADVISOR_HOOK_DISABLED` and `SYSTEM_SKILL_ADVISOR_PLUGIN_DISABLED` to `1`. `--run <n>` takes a whole number from 1 and records it on every line of that run, so two samples of one row stay apart. The `fresh` set reads `behaviour-fresh-prompts.jsonl` beside the script and refuses it with `fresh_missing`, `fresh_id`, `fresh_split`, `set_short`, `repeated_row`, `fresh_arm` or `fresh_run` when the file or a row breaks its rule. Each `g2` run line records the `fixture` folder its prompt named, and `--backfill` replays that folder, or `g2-fixture` beside the log for a line that holds none. `--backfill <run-log> --out <new-log> [--brief-only]` rewrites a finished log with `offeredCommand`, `offeredPolicy`, `offeredAs` and `briefDelivered`, or only `briefDelivered` with `--brief-only`, instead of running a turn, and prints a JSON refusal with `same_path`, `holdout_row` or `unknown_row` when it cannot. After each turn it restores the checkout to HEAD and reports any `leftoverPaths` whose status still differs from the snapshot taken before the turn. Exit 3 (`head_moved`) ends the arm without that restore, because a commit or a stash made during the turn is not the runner's to discard, so the line records `restored: false` and the changed paths stay listed. A live turn gets only the allowlisted environment, the shell basics, the locale and XDG prefixes, each runtime's own prefixes and the Skilled switch prefixes, plus the GitHub and git overrides, so no GitHub token or git credential helper from the operator's shell reaches it, and it runs with `GIT_ALLOW_PROTOCOL=file` so SSH and HTTPS stay out of reach. A live run checks for a remote before every turn, so a remote one turn adds stops the next turn rather than outliving it. Exit 2 refuses instead of running: bad flags, an unknown runtime or set, a holdout or unknown row, a set with fewer rows than it needs (`set_short`), a dirty worktree outside the run log's folder (`worktree_dirty`), a checkout with a git remote (`remote_present`), a checkout without the arena builder's marker (`arena_unprepared`), a selection that mixes started-command rows with others (`mixed_row_arms`), or a Codex arm whose project hook is unapproved (`codex-hooks-unapproved`). Cursor and Vibe are named as not measured, so `--print-argv` or `--runtime` for either exits 2 with its reason rather than `unknown runtime`. |\n | `command-routing-baseline.json` | Captured command routing numbers that `tests/command-routing-slice.vitest.ts` holds the scorer to. |\n | `build-holdout.mjs` | Builds the frozen holdout from three separately authored fixtures that are disjoint from the labeled corpus, copying each row's own label and keeping its provenance in `source_type` and `origin_fixture`. Family-normalized dedup keeps the first occurrence and drops any prompt that collides with the training corpus. `--write` rewrites `holdout-prompts.jsonl`. |\n | `capture-local-native-divergence-ledger.mjs` | Recomputes the strict Python/native top-1 divergence set the ratchet replays. An existing approval is kept only while its prompt hash and both current tops still match, so the output is a capture of live scoring rather than a hand-edited fixture. Scores both scorers against a skill graph indexed fresh into a temporary directory with the clock pinned to the capture day, and `--write` records the result. |\n | `capture-scorer-eval-baseline.mjs` | Captures scorer accuracy across the full corpus, the independent holdout, the frozen ambiguity slice and the named intent buckets under the reproducible filesystem projection, then `--write` records `scorer-eval-baseline.json`. Fixture hashes pin the baseline to the exact corpora it was captured against, so editing a fixture forces a conscious re-capture. `--misses` prints one failing prompt per line. |\n | `derive-ambiguity-slice.mjs` | Derives the frozen ambiguity slice as the corpus rows whose top-2 fused-score margin is at or below `tau`, keeping each row's existing gold label, so a regression that only hurts contested prompts still fails a gate. `--write` rewrites `ambiguity-prompts.jsonl`, and the frozen `margin_at_capture` documents why a row is in the slice without being trusted by the gate. |\n-| `prepare-behaviour-arena.mjs` | Builds the arena a live arm runs in: a clone of the branch tip with no remote and no outer repository, plus the ignored build trees copied in and stamped at copy time so the freshness guard reads them as fresh. It refuses a destination inside a repository or one that already exists, and skips every file whose name begins with `.env` so no secret follows the build output in. When the clone holds no `CLAUDE.md`, the builder writes one holding `@AGENTS.md` and appends `/CLAUDE.md` to `.git/info/exclude`, so Claude Code imports the arena's own rules while the clean-tree check and the head stay as they were. |\n+| `prepare-behaviour-arena.mjs` | Builds the arena a live arm runs in: a clone of the branch tip with no remote and no outer repository, plus the ignored build trees copied in and stamped at copy time so the freshness guard reads them as fresh. It refuses a destination inside a repository or one that already exists, and skips every file whose name begins with `.env` so no secret follows the build output in. It refuses `source_dirty` when the source holds a tracked or untracked edit, `stale_build` when a copied `dist` is older than the newest tracked file in the folder that holds it or in a folder its tsconfig `include` reaches outside it, and `symlink_escape` when a link in a copied tree resolves outside the source or names an absolute path that still resolves inside it. A dotenv file the commit already tracks still arrives with the clone, so the output carries a `trackedDotenv` list naming each one and the marker writes a `tracked-dotenv <path>` line for it. The marker otherwise holds the head, `source clean` and one `built <relPath> <ISO time>` line per copied build tree. When the clone holds no `CLAUDE.md`, the builder writes one holding `@AGENTS.md` and appends `/CLAUDE.md` to `.git/info/exclude`, so Claude Code imports the arena's own rules while the clean-tree check and the head stay as they were. |\n | `reject-unknown-options.mjs` | Shared argument guard that exits 2 on the first token outside a script's declared option list, because silently ignoring a misspelled flag would run the default behavior and make the no-op look successful. |\n | `score-outcome-rerank.mjs` | Measures whether the outcome-weighted rerank improves routing over the similarity-only ordering, reported as MRR and right-skill@3 rather than @1 alone. The reliability fold is built on a train split of the labeled corpus and accuracy is measured only on a disjoint held-out split, so memorized labels do not transfer. Read-only against the skill graph. |\n | `worktree-restore.mjs` | Restores one measured turn's checkout to the snapshot taken before it, leaving a path that was already dirty alone and dropping a staged path HEAD does not know. A second snapshot after the restore reports what still differs, so the caller fails the run instead of claiming a clean worktree. |\ndiff --git a/.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy/command-offer.mjs b/.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy/command-offer.mjs\nindex 197132e0a84..83d8318d5c7 100644\n--- a/.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy/command-offer.mjs\n+++ b/.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy/command-offer.mjs\n@@ -37,6 +37,7 @@ import { dirname, join, resolve } from 'node:path';\n import { fileURLToPath } from 'node:url';\n \n import { loadSession as loadSharedSession } from '../../../../../hooks/shared/session-readers/index.mjs';\n+import { MARK } from './prepare-behaviour-arena.mjs';\n \n const HOOK_RELATIVE = '.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/dist/hooks/claude/user-prompt-submit.js';\n const ADVISOR_CLI_RELATIVE = '.skilled/bin/skill-advisor.cjs';\n@@ -355,7 +356,7 @@ export function trustedCheckout(checkoutDir) {\n   try {\n     return (\n       realpathSync(checkoutDir) === realpathSync(REPO_ROOT)\n-      || existsSync(join(checkoutDir, '.git', 'skilled-behaviour-arena'))\n+      || existsSync(join(checkoutDir, '.git', MARK))\n     );\n   } catch {\n     return false;\ndiff --git a/.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy/run-command-behaviour.mjs b/.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy/run-command-behaviour.mjs\nindex 2cf89e1df07..945ed4c1701 100644\n--- a/.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy/run-command-behaviour.mjs\n+++ b/.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy/run-command-behaviour.mjs\n@@ -32,11 +32,12 @@\n //\n // A live turn is also a turn that can reach the network, so the runner takes the\n // reach away before the model has it. The child gets none of the GitHub tokens, an\n-// empty GitHub CLI config, no credential helper and no terminal to be prompted on.\n-// A live arm additionally refuses to start in a checkout that has any git remote,\n-// because a command wrongly started there could push, tag or publish something the\n-// arm cannot take back. A dry run spawns nothing, so it keeps running in this\n-// checkout and its guards stay about file paths alone.\n+// empty GitHub CLI config, no credential helper and no terminal to be prompted on,\n+// while Git is allowed only the file transport, so SSH and HTTPS stay out of reach\n+// whatever keys HOME holds. A live arm additionally refuses to start in a checkout\n+// that has any git remote, because a command wrongly started there could push, tag\n+// or publish something the arm cannot take back. A dry run spawns nothing, so it\n+// keeps running in this checkout and its guards stay about file paths alone.\n //\n // Usage:\n //   node run-command-behaviour.mjs --print-argv <runtime>\n@@ -54,6 +55,9 @@ import { fileURLToPath } from 'node:url';\n import { dshProjectKey, dshStoreRoot } from '../../../../../hooks/shared/session-readers/dsh.mjs';\n import { loadSession as loadSharedSession } from '../../../../../hooks/shared/session-readers/index.mjs';\n import { backfillRunLog, briefDelivered, codexHookApproval, promptSessionId, replayOffer } from './command-offer.mjs';\n+// The arena builder writes this file into the checkout it made. Its absence is the\n+// proof that a root is an operator's working tree rather than a disposable arena.\n+import { MARK } from './prepare-behaviour-arena.mjs';\n import { restoreTurn, statusSnapshot } from './worktree-restore.mjs';\n \n const HERE = dirname(fileURLToPath(import.meta.url));\n@@ -518,10 +522,6 @@ function locatorFor(runtime, sessionId, reportedId) {\n // lie, and the restore step trusts that snapshot.\n const GIT_PREFIX = ['-c', 'core.fsmonitor=false'];\n \n-// The arena builder writes this file into the checkout it made. Its absence is the\n-// proof that a root is an operator's working tree rather than a disposable arena.\n-const MARK = 'skilled-behaviour-arena';\n-\n function git(args) {\n   const result = spawnSync('git', [...GIT_PREFIX, ...args], {\n     cwd: REPO_ROOT,\n@@ -607,10 +607,14 @@ function gitSnapshot() {\n   };\n }\n \n-// A remote is the one way a turn reaches outside the checkout, so a live arm\n-// refuses to start when one is listed. The builder's marker then proves the root\n-// came from the arena builder, because only that checkout has no remote, no outer\n-// repository and no dotenv file in its copied build trees.\n+// A remote is what a turn could push to by name, so a live arm refuses to start\n+// when one is listed. It is only half the boundary: the push boundary is childEnv's\n+// empty credential helper, empty GH_CONFIG_DIR, GIT_TERMINAL_PROMPT=0 and\n+// GIT_ALLOW_PROTOCOL=file, because a push to an explicit URL needs no remote at all\n+// and the protocol setting keeps SSH and HTTPS out of reach whatever keys HOME\n+// holds. The builder's marker then proves the root came from the arena builder,\n+// because only that checkout has no remote, no outer repository and no dotenv file\n+// in its copied build trees.\n export function liveArmRefusal(root) {\n   const gitAt = (args) => {\n     const result = spawnSync('git', ['-C', root, ...GIT_PREFIX, ...args], {\n@@ -632,6 +636,23 @@ export function liveArmRefusal(root) {\n   return null;\n }\n \n+/**\n+ * Check whether a turn may start where it would run.\n+ *\n+ * A remote one turn adds outlives that turn, so the check runs before every turn\n+ * rather than before the first one only.\n+ *\n+ * @param {string} root - The checkout the turn runs in.\n+ * @param {{ dryRun: boolean }} options - Whether the turn spawns a model.\n+ * @returns {object | null} The refusal, or null when the turn may start.\n+ */\n+export function turnRefusal(root, { dryRun }) {\n+  // A dry run spawns nothing and keeps running in this checkout, so it has no\n+  // turn to guard.\n+  if (dryRun) return null;\n+  return liveArmRefusal(root);\n+}\n+\n // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n // Prompt source and run log\n // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n@@ -1033,6 +1054,8 @@ export function childEnv(ghConfig, arm) {\n   }\n   env.GH_CONFIG_DIR = ghConfig;\n   env.GIT_TERMINAL_PROMPT = '0';\n+  // Git's network transports would let a turn push with whatever identity HOME holds.\n+  env.GIT_ALLOW_PROTOCOL = 'file';\n   Object.assign(env, GIT_CREDENTIAL_OVERRIDES);\n   return env;\n }\n@@ -1263,16 +1286,18 @@ function writeArgv(runtimeName) {\n // fires, so a broken precondition never spends another model call.\n async function runTurn(job, options, exempt, isFirst) {\n   const before = { status: statusPaths(), ...gitSnapshot() };\n+  // A live turn can push, so it may only start in the arena the builder made: no\n+  // remote it could push to by name and the builder's marker in place. The push\n+  // boundary is childEnv's empty credential helper, empty GH_CONFIG_DIR,\n+  // GIT_TERMINAL_PROMPT=0 and GIT_ALLOW_PROTOCOL=file, because a push to an explicit\n+  // URL needs no remote and the protocol setting keeps SSH and HTTPS out of reach\n+  // whatever keys HOME holds. A dry run spawns nothing and keeps running in this\n+  // checkout.\n+  const refusal = turnRefusal(REPO_ROOT, { dryRun: options.dryRun });\n+  if (refusal !== null) {\n+    return { status: EXIT_REFUSED, refusal };\n+  }\n   if (isFirst) {\n-    // A live turn can push, so it may only start in the arena the builder made: no\n-    // remote to push to and the builder's marker in place. A dry run spawns nothing\n-    // and keeps running in this checkout.\n-    if (!options.dryRun) {\n-      const refusal = liveArmRefusal(REPO_ROOT);\n-      if (refusal !== null) {\n-        return { status: EXIT_REFUSED, refusal };\n-      }\n-    }\n     const dirty = dirtyPaths(before.status, exempt);\n     if (dirty.length > 0) {\n       return {\n"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"command\":\"cd /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research && git -c core.fsmonitor=false diff -U3 2a1a42263cc d21dbc1cafe -- .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy/run-command-behaviour.mjs .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy/command-offer.mjs .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy/README.md\"}"},"time":{"start":1794781046458,"end":1794781047437}}}}
